title: Change part of a body without rewriting the whole one
created: 2026-09-18T17:55:07Z
updated: 2026-09-18T17:55:07Z
effort: M
area: cli
handoff:

The worst row in the audit, because the workaround is more dangerous than the
gap. edit writes a body three ways: an editor, which an agent cannot drive;
-a, which appends; and -b, which replaces the whole thing. So changing one
paragraph non-interactively means reconstructing the entire body and piping
it back, and anything left out is silently deleted.

That is how 24 and 28 were corrected in 99ebca6 — extract with awk, replace a
passage in a temp file, pipe it all back — and it survived review only
because the tickets were short enough to check by eye. The tickets in this
tracker are no longer short.

The failure has no signal. A truncated body is a valid ticket: check has
nothing to say about it, the file parses, and the loss shows up whenever
someone next reads the ticket and the paragraph they remember is gone. Every
other manual idiom in this audit fails loudly.

What would remove it, roughly in order of how much they assume:

- A way to replace a passage by match rather than by rewriting the file.
- A way to edit in place through a filter, so the caller transforms what is
  there instead of reconstructing it.
- Accepting the risk and making the reconstruction safe: have -b refuse a
  body dramatically shorter than the one it replaces unless forced. Cheap,
  and catches the actual failure rather than preventing it.

The last one is worth taking seriously even if a better command lands, since
it costs little and the failure it catches is the one that has already
happened twice.

Done when changing a paragraph does not require reconstructing the body, or
the reconstruction cannot silently lose one, and the suite covers a body that
would have been truncated.
